#   2041 2005 2026 2039 2024 2035 2008 2044 2013 2032 2028
#   2026 2003 2026 2033 2025 2028 2028 2029 2023 2023
# max=2044 (round 7)  min=2003 (round 12)  spread=41  n=21
# The tree's denominator (7978) is unchanged from Task 1's merged-tree measurement,
# but this is a DIFFERENT measurement, not a re-run of it: Task 1's floor candidate
# was 2048 on the tree before Tasks 3/4 added two tests (row 29's and row 30's), and
# this one is 2044 -- 4 statements lower, on a tree with slightly more covered, not a
# discrepancy between two measurements of the same thing.
#
# HOW TO MOVE THIS FLOOR (read before running --write-floor).
#
# THESE ARE TWO DIFFERENT KINDS OF MOVE, and only one of them needs the campaign
# below. A `missing` move changes what the ratchet PERMITS and can only be justified
# by a real measurement of the spread -- that is steps 1-5. A `shape`/`modules`/
# `rcfile`-only re-baseline (the module list or the rcfile changed, deliberately, and
# `missing` is UNCHANGED from what the previous floor already recorded) is not a claim
# about the spread at all -- it is telling the gate "the thing being measured is
# different now, here is its new identity." That needs exactly ONE clean local or CI
# run and a single `--write-floor --shape-only`, WITHOUT steps 1, 2, 4 or 5's
# multi-round census: there is no new `missing` value to earn through repeated
# measurement, because none is being claimed, and `--shape-only` cannot write one --
# it updates `shape`, `modules`, `module_count` and `rcfile` only, and leaves
# `statements`, `missing`, `percent`, `measured` and `runs` byte-identical to
# whatever they already were. Say which kind of move this is in the PR either way --
# "re-baselined with --shape-only, missing unchanged" is a one-line, one-run PR;
# "raised the floor to N, campaign attached" is not.
#
# `--shape-only` is the answer to a question this file's own plain `--write-floor`
# already declined once (see step 4 below): can the tool do this safely without a
# hand-edit? The two are not the same request. Plain `--write-floor` was asked to
# accept an explicit `missing` VALUE, which has to thread through the regression
# check (which run's figure counts as "worse than the floor"?) and the percent
# computation (recomputed against which value?) -- real machinery, declined on
# purpose. `--shape-only` writes FEWER fields instead: it never touches `missing` or
# `percent` at all, so neither question has anywhere to attach. A hand-edit procedure
# for this case was tried first and rejected: a maintainer following prose instead of
# running one command is the failure mode this guard exists to prevent one level
# further up, and the two ways to get it wrong are silent and both green -- run plain
# `--write-floor` for a shape-only move and the campaign's `missing`/`runs`
# provenance is silently overwritten by whatever one run just produced (measured:
# 2050->2006, runs 15->0, on a real floor, in the course of writing this fix); hand-
# restore those fields afterward and forget, and nothing checks that you did.
#
# What follows is the multi-round procedure for when `missing` itself is moving.
# `--write-floor` (without `--shape-only`) writes the figure from the run it JUST
# took -- one run, not the worst of N -- because the floor policy (worst of >=12
# rounds under a stopping rule; see the top of this file) lives in the stopping-rule
# PROCEDURE, not in the script. The two are not the same thing by design, and
# `--write-floor` cannot know it is being run for the Nth time in a campaign versus
# once, so it never guesses at "worst so far" on your behalf. The exact sequence,
# every time `missing` legitimately moves:
#   1. Measure IN CI, not only locally: dispatch backend-tests.yml on the branch
#      repeatedly and read `this run missing=` from each coverage-gate job, until the
#      MAXIMUM has been unchanged for >=6 consecutive rounds, >=12 total. Record every
#      round, in order -- a bare min/max cannot show the rule was met. Local rounds via
#      scripts/coverage_live_path_isolated.sh --report are still worth taking (they are
#      far faster to iterate on, and they attribute a change per file), but they do NOT
#      set the number: this floor was first set from a 21-round LOCAL census whose
#      maximum the very first CI run exceeded. The floor must be measured where it is
#      enforced. Note backend-tests.yml's concurrency group is per-ref with
#      cancel-in-progress, so CI rounds on one branch are sequential, not parallel.
#   2. Take max(runs). That number, not any single run's own figure, is what belongs
#      in `missing` below.
#   3. Run plain --write-floor once (writable checkout or a scratch rw container --
#      /repo is read-only in the standard test-hook container) to get the correct
#      FILE FORMAT, shape, statements, modules, module_count and rcfile fields
#      machine-produced -- and scripts/coverage_live_path.floor.modules rewritten to
#      match. Commit that file alongside this one; it is what lets a future --gate
#      mismatch name files, not just hashes.
#   4. Hand-edit `missing` to max(runs) from step 2 if it differs from what
#      --write-floor's own run produced (it usually will -- one run is rarely the
#      worst of N), and recompute `percent` = (1 - missing/statements) * 100 to match.
#      This hand-edit is the honest, documented step for THIS case, not a workaround:
#      teaching plain --write-floor to accept an explicit `missing` override was
#      considered for this PR and declined, for the threading reasons above -- a
#      maintainer action taken rarely and by hand, with no shape-only shortcut
#      available because a real `missing` move has no "fewer fields" version.
#   5. Re-run --gate from the ordinary READ-ONLY test-hook container -- the shape CI
#      actually runs it in -- and confirm it exits 0 against the new floor before
#      committing.
shape=per-container/v1-sysmon
statements=8073
missing=1525
percent=81.11
measured=2026-09-13
runs=12
modules=8cb5c65dac3e
module_count=38
rcfile=3af0a78b9b6f
